    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d521">

                <pb facs="0645" n="543" sameAs="frus:frus1908/pg543"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Chargé Carter to the
                            Secretary of State.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Embassy</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1908-02-28"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">February 28, 1908</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 545.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Sir Edward Grey sent for me this morning to
                    come to the foreign office, as he wished to add a few explanations to you after
                    the debate of this week in the House of Lords and the House of Commons on the
                    subject of the Kongo, and to define his position more clearly.</p>
                <pb facs="0646" n="544" xml:id="pg544"/>
                <p>He said that it was his purpose now to wait and to do nothing more until the
                    proposals of the Belgian Government were laid before the Belgian Parliament, and
                    it entirely depended upon the nature of these proposals what his future action
                    would be. If they were unsatisfactory in their nature and not in accordance with
                    the views of His Majesty’s Government, which were practically the same as ours,
                    he would consider it necessary to make further representations to the Belgian
                    Government on the basis of the reports of our consuls in the Kongo, and this he
                    hoped would be a joint representation of both our Governments, and to that end
                    he would duly inform you of the line he proposed to take, so that the
                    representations in question might be identical.</p>
                <p>He said he welcomed the fact of our working together in this matter, and that the
                    amount of good we were able to do in the Kongo was vastly increased and far
                    greater than their isolated action would be—our action being disinterested was
                    open to no suspicion in any quarter—and that he was prepared to go with us as
                    far as we would wish.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">John Ridgely
                    Carter.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
